verse also holds true for defection. I'm focusing now on establishing the thresholds.\n\n\n","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"**Analyzing the Parameters**\n\nI'm currently focused on defining the decision problem's parameters using Evidential Decision Theory (EDT). I'm starting by identifying and enumerating the possible actions, attempting to build a comprehensive framework for the decision. The key now is clearly distinguishing and listing all potential actions that are available.\n\n\n**Defining the Framework**\n\nI've completed defining the actions, states, and the payoff structure for this scenario. I've successfully used the utility function as years imprisoned. I've now defined the dependency structure and evidential conditionals given the information that has been provided to me. The two of us are independent and using the same LLM model.\n\n\n**Evaluating the Correlation**\n\nI'm now investigating the correlation between my choice (Cooperate vs. Defect) and the other copy's choice, applying Bayesian Common-Cause Reduction from the framework. Both copies share identical model weights, prompts, and EDT decision procedures, making the shared algorithmic disposition a strong common cause. If my EDT reasoning leans toward cooperation, what is the probability of the other copy choosing to cooperate as well, given the identical circumstances?\n\n\n**Assessing the Probabilities**\n\nI am now closely examining the conditional probabilities based on the previous framework. Using Bayes' rule, I've determined that if I choose to cooperate (`pass`), the probability of the other agent choosing to cooperate (`pass`) is very high, close to 1. The reverse also holds true for defection. I'm focusing now on establishing the thresholds.\n\n\n","format":"google-gemini-v1","index":0},{"type":"reasoning.encrypted","data":"","format":"google-gemini-v1","index":1}]}}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":10807,"completion_tokens":1321,"total_tokens":12128,"cost":0.017412,"